<BaseLayout title="About The Big Hauler: Straight Answers for the Road" description="What The Big Hauler is and who it's for.">
  <h1>About The Big Hauler</h1>
  <p>
    The Big Hauler publishes practical, fact-first guides for people who drive trucks for a living: company drivers, owner-operators, and small fleet owners. ELD compliance, dispatch, maintenance, fuel, insurance, and the business side of trucking.
  </p>
  <h2>Why we exist</h2>
  <p>
    Most trucking advice falls into two extremes: generic small-business content that ignores how the industry actually works, or expensive consultants. We publish the middle ground: concrete, current information on the rules, the tools, and the costs of running a truck, written for people who are just starting out.
  </p>
  <h2>What we cover</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Compliance</strong>: ELD rules, hours of service, DOT inspections, IFTA, and what the FMCSA actually requires</li>
    <li><strong>Operations</strong>: TMS and dispatch software, load boards, brokers, factoring, and quick pay</li>
    <li><strong>The truck</strong>: maintenance, diesel engines, tires, air brakes, trailers, and what repairs should cost</li>
    <li><strong>Money</strong>: fuel management, insurance, financing, taxes, and the numbers that make or break an owner-operator</li>
    <li><strong>The career</strong>: CDL requirements, endorsements, driving school, and getting your first job</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Our standards</h2>
  <p>
    We update pricing and regulation information regularly. When a guide mentions a product, service, or rule, we state the date it was last checked. We do not invent facts: if something is uncertain, we say so.
  </p>
  <p>
    Some articles contain affiliate links. That never changes our recommendations, and it is always disclosed. See our <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">Affiliate Disclosure</a>.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
